Lab: Introduction to Kubernetes and OpenShift
Objective
Use the OpenShift Web Console to find important information about the cluster configuration.
You will learn to:
•	Log in to the OpenShift web console
•	View the cluster version
•	Check alert severity filters
•	Inspect routes and labels
•	View storage classes
•	Check installed operators
________________________________________
1. Start the Lab Environment
On the workstation machine, run:
lab start intro-review
This ensures the OpenShift cluster is running and ready.
________________________________________
2. Open the OpenShift Web Console
Open a browser and go to:
https://console-openshift-console.apps.ocp4.example.com
________________________________________
3. Log in to the Console
1.	Select Red Hat Identity Management
2.	Enter the following credentials:
Field	Value
Username	admin
Password	redhatocp
3.	Click Log in
After login you will see the OpenShift dashboard.
________________________________________
4. View the OpenShift Cluster Version
Navigate to:
Home → Overview
Scroll down to the Details section.
Look for:
OpenShift Version
This shows the version of Red Hat OpenShift Container Platform running on the cluster.
________________________________________
5. View Alert Severity Types
Go to:
Observe → Alerting
Click the Filter dropdown.
You will see alert severity options such as:
•	Critical
•	Warning
•	Info
These are used to classify cluster alerts.
________________________________________
6. View Labels for the thanos-querier Route
Navigate to:
Networking → Routes
In the search box, type:
thanos
Click the route:
thanos-querier
Scroll down to find the Labels section.
Labels are key-value metadata used to organize and identify resources.
________________________________________
7. View Storage Classes
Navigate to:
Storage → StorageClasses
Here you can see the available storage classes in the cluster.
Storage classes define how persistent storage is provisioned.
Examples may include:
•	gp2
•	standard
•	local-storage
________________________________________
8. View Installed Operators
Go to:
Operators → Installed Operators
This page lists operators currently installed in the cluster.
Operators help automate:
•	Application deployment
•	Updates
•	Lifecycle management
Examples include:
•	OpenShift Monitoring Operator
•	Kubernetes NMState Operator
•	OpenShift Logging Operator
________________________________________
9. Finish the Lab
After completing the tasks, run:
lab finish intro-review
This cleans up the lab environment.
________________________________________
Key Things Learned
You practiced how to:
•	Navigate the OpenShift Web Console
•	Identify the cluster version
•	Explore alert monitoring
•	Inspect routes and labels
•	Check storage configuration
•	View installed operators
